{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795609928,"nonce":"TExJUGfyJhHDICnxPui8V0iCIVk=","title":"A rule for two dynamic attributes keeps applying after one goes","message":"`.t[data-a][data-b]` with both attributes following signals (`attr_when`, `class_when`) applies when both are on, and stays applied when the one that came first is removed. The same holds across an ancestor: `.p[data-x] .c[data-y]`.\n\nCause: the rules a class brings are kept as that class's layer. A rule naming two classes is held by whichever came last, and removing the other only dropped its own layer.\n\nFound as the rich text editor's scroll lane keeping both edge fades at either end (26002b452b75be9fe98ed2910cd304adffc0646930cfabb9a4f22c9e719c598b).\n\nFix: on removal, drop from every layer of the node, and of its descendants, the rules that no longer match. Tests in `class_when` and `class_when_descendants` fail before it.\n\nA rule that stops matching because a class is added (through `:not`) still waits for the next full style pass, as the module documents.","files":null}]}